+This is a pure-Python reimplementation of `packignore`, the legacy exclusion
+engine. It reproduces packignore's actual behaviour, which differs from
+docs/PACKIGNORE.md in several places:
+
+- Lines are split with str.splitlines() and stripped of surrounding
+  whitespace. Lines starting with ";" are comments; "#" is not special.
+- "!" negates; whatever follows it is used verbatim (no further stripping).
+- Only "*", "?" and "**" are wildcards. "[", "]" and "\\" are literal.
+  "**" is special only as a whole path segment, where it matches zero to
+  three segments (so "a/**" also matches "a" itself, but not "a/b/c/d/e");
+  elsewhere it acts like "*".
+- A file's verdict is decided by the deepest path level (the file itself,
+  then each enclosing directory, innermost first) matched by any pattern.
+  At that level, patterns from the innermost .packignore win; within one
+  file, the pattern with the most non-wildcard characters ("*", "?" and "/"
+  don't count) wins, and the later pattern breaks ties.
+- A .packignore inside an excluded directory is not read.
+- Files with any execute bit set, or with "@generated" in their first line,
+  are excluded unless a pattern matches the file itself (a pattern matching
+  only an enclosing directory doesn't override this).
